    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-40277",
      "knowledge_point_id": "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series",
      "problem": "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$\n\nII. $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$\n\nIII. $-8+16-32+64-⋯$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-2}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-8+16-32+64-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and II converge.",
